    20	
    21	**V1 claim (one sentence).** *A V1 receipt lets anyone holding it establish, without Tessera, that these exact
    22	bytes were presented to the Tessera V1 service and signed by its two keys K = {K_kms, K_gpg}, that the
    23	authority of those keys for domain D was shown at issuance through DNSSEC and an anchored repository
    24	publication, and that the signed bytes existed no later than time T. It does this with a typed verdict that
    25	says what was not established.*
    26	
    27	| # | Atomic sub-claim | Non-claim kept beside it |
    28	|---|---|---|
    29	| C1 | **Bytes.** The bytes the verifier rebuilds from the bundle are exactly the framed bytes signed at issuance, with one encoding and no ambiguity at the boundaries. | Says nothing about meaning or truth (A3 §A3.1). |
    30	| C2 | **Presentation.** Those bytes were presented at some issuance event of the V1 service. This is existential: it holds for some event, not a unique one. | Says nothing about who submitted them (scenario f), or whether a replay suits the transaction (R-1.5). |
    31	| C3 | **Signature.** Both required keys signed exactly this frame. A missing, stripped or substituted signature fails, and so does a DSKS key. | Nothing about the signing-key lifecycle after T. |
    32	| C4 | **Authority.** At issuance, K was published for domain D through two named channels: (i) a DNSSEC chain whose owner name is D's, and (ii) an anchored git publication. The verifier fixes the required channel set; the bundle does not. | Not that the domain holder was honest. Capture of the whole author sphere is the R-2.15 residual. |
    33	| C5 | **Time.** The framed bytes existed no later than T. T is the earliest verified Bitcoin attestation in the receipt's own OTS proof. That anchor sits within [declared − ε, declared + δ] and was confirmed at depth k within δ. | Gives no lower bound and no priority (A7 §A7.4). |
    34	| C6 | **Verifiable without Tessera.** The verdict is a pure function of three things: the bundle, the published V1 verifier, and that verifier's distributed trust configuration (DNSSEC root keys, the repository key, a Bitcoin checkpoint). It needs no network and no help from the authors. | Keeping the bundle is the holder's job (custody). |
