   337	
   338	## 3. Objects and formats
   339	
   340	Scope: the byte-level and structural contract — what an object is, what fields it carries, who
   341	writes each field, who checks it. Verdict semantics are §5; standing semantics are §6. Requirements
   342	carry the source's own normative force; descriptive text is not upgraded to a MUST here.
   343	
   344	*Provenance: every `[REGISTERED A3 …]` tag in this section rests on Amendment 3's adoption at `8ae4720`; see §0.1.*
   345	
   346	---
   347	
   348	### 3.1 Payload and canonical form
   349	
   350	| Field / rule | Type / domain | Who sets | Who checks | Source |
   351	|---|---|---|---|---|
   352	| `payload` | JCS (RFC 8785) bytes over a JSON value in the accepted domain | issuer | verifier, by independent reconstruction | A1 §A1.2 P8 |
   353	| canonicalization version | version identifier, recorded per layer; unknown version rejected | issuer | verifier | prereg §4.3; A1 P8 |
   354	| non-double values | string-encoded, never a JSON number | issuer / ingestion boundary | verifier (rejection list) | prereg §4.3 |
   355	| accepted domain | closed by explicit rejection; no normalization | — | verifier | A1 §A1.2 P8 |
   356	
   357	**R-3.1** The canonical form binds to RFC 8785 (JSON Canonicalization Scheme, JCS) rather than a
   358	bespoke `canonical()`; JCS is cited as Informational, not standards-track, adopted with the
   359	precision rule below closing its gap. [REGISTERED prereg §4.3, docs/phase-0-prereg.md:496-506]
   360	
   361	**R-3.2** Any value not exactly a double (hashes, large/256-bit integers, blockchain addresses,
   362	opaque identifiers) is string-encoded, never a JSON number.
   363	[REGISTERED prereg §4.3, docs/phase-0-prereg.md:502-506]
   364	
   365	**R-3.3** `canonical()` is injective on its accepted payload domain and the encoding is
   366	boundary-unambiguous; injectivity over that domain is discharged by direct proof.
   367	[REGISTERED A1 §A1.2 P8, docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-1.md:255-277]
   368	
   369	**R-3.4** The accepted domain is closed by explicit rejection: anything outside it is refused, never
   370	"helpfully" normalized. Rejection is required, at minimum, for duplicate object names;
   371	`NaN`/`Infinity`; numbers not exactly representable as IEEE 754 doubles; `-0` and numeric edge
   372	cases; non-I-JSON strings; inputs relying on Unicode normalization assumptions; unknown
   373	canonicalization versions; trailing bytes; type-tag mismatch; length-prefix mismatch.
   374	[REGISTERED A1 §A1.2 P8, docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-1.md:264-273]
   375	
   376	**R-3.5** The canonical form is frozen and explicitly versioned: it never changes silently, any
   377	change is a new versioned canonical form, and every receipt records which version it used.
   378	[REGISTERED prereg §4.3, docs/phase-0-prereg.md:486-494]
   379	
   380	**R-3.6** Every canonicalization version has byte-for-byte golden examples and canonicalization test
   381	vectors are part of the public verifier conformance suite; golden vectors evidence the positive
   382	domain and the rejection suite the negative domain, both H1a obligations.
   383	[REGISTERED prereg §4.3, docs/phase-0-prereg.md:508-512; A1 §A1.2 P8, docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-1.md:273-276]
   384	
   385	**R-3.7** The claim made for the length binding is boundary-unambiguity; no blanket
   386	"length-extension-resistant" claim is made. [REGISTERED prereg §4.3, docs/phase-0-prereg.md:514-517]
   387	
   388	**R-3.8** Schema validation rejects raw JSON numerics exceeding IEEE 754 exact range before
   389	canonicalization, so string-encoding is enforced at the ingestion boundary rather than assumed of
   390	clients. [DOCKET 4 — candidate, not registered, docs/band-1-docket.md:32-36] [OPEN — §11 O-02]
   391	
   392	**Two byte strings, separated.** The **canonical payload bytes** are `canonical(payload)`: the JCS
   393	(RFC 8785) serialization under the precision rule above. The **signed frame** is the framed envelope
   394	of §3.2, whose fourth field *is* the canonical payload bytes. Signatures and anchors commit to the
   395	signed frame, and the length is a frame field. The original §4.3 sentence "Length is bound into the
   396	canonical bytes (not a side field)" (`docs/phase-0-prereg.md:492-494`) is superseded by A1 P8, as
   397	A1 §A1.8 already implies, and is read from Amendment 7 on as "the length is bound into the signed
   398	bytes"; the original is not edited. Golden vectors are owed for the two strings separately (P8: the
   399	canonical payload bytes; the frame layout: the signed frame).
   400	[RULED (author) 2026-09-14; instrument A7 §A7.1, PROPOSED; §12 C-03]
   401	
   402	---
   403	
   404	### 3.2 The frame
   405	
   406	| Field | Type / domain | Who sets | Who checks | Source |
   407	|---|---|---|---|---|
   408	| `type_tag` | domain-separation tag from P7's enumerated set | issuer | verifier; mismatch rejected | A1 P7, P8 |
   409	| `canonicalization_version` | version identifier | issuer | verifier; unknown version rejected | A1 §A1.2 P8 |
   410	| `payload_length` | length prefix the frame supplies, JCS carrying no length header | issuer | verifier; mismatch rejected | A1 §A1.2 P8 |
   411	| `payload` | the JCS bytes | issuer | verifier | A1 §A1.2 P8 |
   412	| binary layout, widths, endianness | not fixed | — | — | A1 P8 [OPEN — §11 O-01] |
   413	
   414	*Terms.* The **signed frame** is the four-field envelope tabulated above; the **canonical payload
   415	bytes** are its fourth field, `canonical(payload)`. `payload_length` is a field of the frame and not
   416	part of the canonical payload bytes; signatures and anchors commit to the signed frame.
   417	[RULED (author) 2026-09-14; instrument A7 §A7.1, PROPOSED; §12 C-03]
   418	
   419	**R-3.9** Signatures and anchors commit to a framed envelope
   420	`type_tag || canonicalization_version || payload_length || payload`, where `payload` is the JCS
   421	bytes; the issuer constructs the frame, the verifier reconstructs it independently and rejects any
   422	mismatch. [REGISTERED A1 §A1.2 P8, docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-1.md:255-264]
   423	
   424	**R-3.10** The exact binary layout is fixed, with golden vectors, before the Band 1 freeze; it is an
   425	outstanding obligation, not a settled specification.
   426	[REGISTERED A1 §A1.2 P8, docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-1.md:262-264] [OPEN — §11 O-01]
   427	
   428	**R-3.11** The signed bytes include, at minimum: domain-separation tag, object type (per P7's
   429	enumeration), algorithm identifier, issuer identity, key fingerprint, manifest hash, and
   430	canonicalization version. [REGISTERED A1 §A1.2 P3, docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-1.md:140-144]
   431	
   432	**R-3.12** For a multi-signer attestation the six non-fingerprint fields of every required signer's
   433	frame — object type, algorithm, identity, manifest hash, canonicalization version, payload — are
   434	equal across signer slots; only the key-fingerprint field differs. Reading (a), common attested
   435	content in separate signer-specific frames, is registered; if reading (b), one shared frame, was
   436	meant, P3's field list and P8's frame change.
   437	[RULED (author) A5 §A5.4 — ADOPTED (author), docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-5.md:165-190 (the (a)/(b) fork at :165-179; the six-field sentence at :179-183)] [RULED — §12 C-15, A7 §A7.9]
   438	
   439	**R-3.12a** P8's four-field signed frame and A5 §A5.4's seven signer-specific bindings (object type,
   440	algorithm, identity, manifest hash, canonicalization version, payload, key fingerprint) both stand
   441	and are not in conflict, *provided* an **encoding map** shows where each symbolic binding lives in
   442	bytes: as an envelope field (type tag, canonicalization version, payload), inside the canonical
   443	payload under P3's obligations (the A3 §A3.6.1 precedent for the algorithm identifier, docket 25's
   444	for the forward link), or in the manifest tuple the frame commits to by hash. The map must also
