   337	
   338	## 3. Objects and formats
   339	
   340	Scope: the byte-level and structural contract — what an object is, what fields it carries, who
   341	writes each field, who checks it. Verdict semantics are §5; standing semantics are §6. Requirements
   342	carry the source's own normative force; descriptive text is not upgraded to a MUST here.
   343	
   344	*Provenance: every `[REGISTERED A3 …]` tag in this section rests on Amendment 3's adoption at `8ae4720`; see §0.1.*
   345	
   346	---
   347	
   348	### 3.1 Payload and canonical form
   349	
   350	| Field / rule | Type / domain | Who sets | Who checks | Source |
   351	|---|---|---|---|---|
   352	| `payload` | JCS (RFC 8785) bytes over a JSON value in the accepted domain | issuer | verifier, by independent reconstruction | A1 §A1.2 P8 |
   353	| canonicalization version | version identifier, recorded per layer; unknown version rejected | issuer | verifier | prereg §4.3; A1 P8 |
   354	| non-double values | string-encoded, never a JSON number | issuer / ingestion boundary | verifier (rejection list) | prereg §4.3 |
   355	| accepted domain | closed by explicit rejection; no normalization | — | verifier | A1 §A1.2 P8 |
   356	
   357	**R-3.1** The canonical form binds to RFC 8785 (JSON Canonicalization Scheme, JCS) rather than a
   358	bespoke `canonical()`; JCS is cited as Informational, not standards-track, adopted with the
   359	precision rule below closing its gap. [REGISTERED prereg §4.3, docs/phase-0-prereg.md:496-506]
   360	
   361	**R-3.2** Any value not exactly a double (hashes, large/256-bit integers, blockchain addresses,
   362	opaque identifiers) is string-encoded, never a JSON number.
   363	[REGISTERED prereg §4.3, docs/phase-0-prereg.md:502-506]
   364	
   365	**R-3.3** `canonical()` is injective on its accepted payload domain and the encoding is
   366	boundary-unambiguous; injectivity over that domain is discharged by direct proof.
   367	[REGISTERED A1 §A1.2 P8, docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-1.md:255-277]
   368	
   369	**R-3.4** The accepted domain is closed by explicit rejection: anything outside it is refused, never
   370	"helpfully" normalized. Rejection is required, at minimum, for duplicate object names;
   371	`NaN`/`Infinity`; numbers not exactly representable as IEEE 754 doubles; `-0` and numeric edge
   372	cases; non-I-JSON strings; inputs relying on Unicode normalization assumptions; unknown
   373	canonicalization versions; trailing bytes; type-tag mismatch; length-prefix mismatch.
   374	[REGISTERED A1 §A1.2 P8, docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-1.md:264-273]
   375	
   376	**R-3.5** The canonical form is frozen and explicitly versioned: it never changes silently, any
   377	change is a new versioned canonical form, and every receipt records which version it used.
   378	[REGISTERED prereg §4.3, docs/phase-0-prereg.md:486-494]
   379	
   380	**R-3.6** Every canonicalization version has byte-for-byte golden examples and canonicalization test
   381	vectors are part of the public verifier conformance suite; golden vectors evidence the positive
   382	domain and the rejection suite the negative domain, both H1a obligations.
   383	[REGISTERED prereg §4.3, docs/phase-0-prereg.md:508-512; A1 §A1.2 P8, docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-1.md:273-276]
   384	
   385	**R-3.7** The claim made for the length binding is boundary-unambiguity; no blanket
