   102	**What a standard choice implies.**
   103	- The value-preserving reading is the one the precision rule's own examples point at (hashes, 256-bit
   104	  integers, addresses).
   105	- It accepts the phase-2 SBOM.
   106	- It refuses exactly the inputs JCS would alter.
   107	- Adopting it means R-3.4 must say "numbers whose value is not preserved by RFC 8785 serialization",
   108	  and that "no normalization" means no *value* change.
   109	- The three implementations agree on output bytes for all 24 Appendix B vectors, but not on which
   110	  inputs they accept. Only R-3.6's negative vectors can pin that. "Bind to an existing standard"
   111	  alone does not.
   112	- The phase-1 subnormal rule (G-03b) refuses an input RFC 8785 itself treats as ordinary.
   113	
   114	### 2. Signatures
   115	
   116	**Exactly which bytes are signed.**
   117	- **KMS ECDSA_SHA_256, RAW:** ECDSA over SHA-256(frame). KMS sees the frame.
   118	- **KMS DIGEST:** the caller sends SHA-256(frame), and KMS never sees the frame. DIGEST is forced
   119	  above 4096 B, and the KMS-backed OpenPGP slot always uses it. The signature still verifies over the
   120	  frame with an ordinary verifier. So "signs the frame bytes" holds at the verifier, but the
   121	  independent custody domain checks nothing about content.
   122	- **RSA-PSS:** randomized, salt 32.
   123	- **OpenPGP v4 (gpg):** the hash input is `frame ‖ hashed_header ‖ 0x04 0xFF ‖ len`. The
   124	  hashed header holds the signer-chosen creation time and the issuer fingerprint.
   125	  - ECDSA signs that hash. Raw ECDSA over the frame fails, as the tests show.
   126	  - For gpg 2.4's default ed25519 key, Ed25519 signs the 32 *digest bytes*. It verifies over neither
   127	    the frame nor frame ‖ trailer, only over SHA-256(frame ‖ trailer).
   128	
   129	**Does "signs the frame bytes" hold?** Only if the verification primitive is "OpenPGP signature over
   130	document fb". If it is, V19 holds for the GPG slot. The trailer is then signed metadata outside the
   131	frame, and OpenPGP verifiers use its creation time to judge key expiry and revocation (R-3.39).
   132	
   133	**Does the same-algorithm rule block a realistic pairing?** Yes (P1 and P2 `INVALID` on V13, V16, V19,
   134	V24). Only two pairings pass:
   135	
   136	| Pairing | Result | Cost |
   137	|---|---|---|
   138	| P1 KMS ECDSA + gpg OpenPGP (P-256) | `INVALID` | — |
   139	| P2 KMS ECDSA + gpg OpenPGP (Ed25519, gpg's default) | `INVALID` | — |
   140	| P3 KMS ECDSA + gpg-agent `PKSIGN` over SHA-256(frame) | `VALID_STRICT` | The GPG slot is a raw DER signature; `gpg --verify` cannot check it. The "portable WHO layer" is gone |
   141	| P4 KMS-backed OpenPGP + gpg OpenPGP | `VALID_STRICT` (the de-stubbed world) | KMS signs an uninterpretable digest. The KMS key needs an invented OpenPGP creation time, and its v4 fingerprint (the one DNS and git would publish) depends on it |
   142	
   143	**Malleability** (finding 1). This holds for ECDSA (both KMS and OpenPGP) and for every OpenPGP
   144	signature through its unhashed area. It does not hold for RSA-PSS (randomized, but only the signer
   145	can re-sign) or for Ed25519 under RFC 8032 (`cryptography` rejects s + L).
   146	
   147	**DSKS** stayed emulated. Two real-mechanism facts are relevant:
   148	- OpenPGP v4 signatures already hash the issuer's fingerprint. That is an existing key-binding
   149	  defence the spec does not use.
   150	- An *inline* OpenPGP message (`gpg --sign`, gpg's default mode) carries the signed content inside
   151	  the signature. That is the "message recovery" phase 1 had to invent (G-41) for the C-C5 mutant to
   152	  be expressible. A GPG slot built on inline signing would make that bug class natural.
