   154	### 3. Anchor (OpenTimestamps)
   155	
   156	Test vectors: `timestamps/anchored/03cd3db…` and `fcaa11b…` (copied to `fixtures/`), with mainnet
   157	headers 956576–956644 and 959013–959094 fetched once from Blockstream's API. The tests check every
   158	header's PoW and linkage, so the API is not trusted.
   159	
   160	| Spec | What the OTS proof gives |
   161	|---|---|
   162	| "exactly one anchor proof ... together with the anchor transaction's id" (R-3.42) | One file, **four** Bitcoin attestations (heights 956582/586/620/639), four distinct txids, plus four `PendingAttestation`s. The txid is not a field: it is implicit in the operations. Each transaction is a calendar's aggregation transaction, shared with every other stamp in that round |
   163	| `anchor_time` = "the Bitcoin block time of the OTS anchor" (R-3.44) | Four candidates, spanning 8.6 h (03cd3db) and 12.6 h (fcaa11b). This prototype takes the **earliest** verified attestation (G-55), the strongest true "existed by". The proof is outside the signed bytes, so a presenter can prune attestations. Under "earliest", pruning can only move `anchor_time` later; under any other rule the presenter chooses it. Which one is used decides V33–V35 near their bounds and decides REV (R-5.21) |
   164	| k-confirmation depth (V36), `confirmed_at` (V35) | Not in the proof. Headers h … h+k−1 must come from the bundle or a store |
   165	| Header store (R-2.19, V38 "not specified") | Authentication needs PoW, linkage, retarget rules and a pinned checkpoint. Two policies are built: phase 1's "pin every header" and R-3.48's candidate "checkpoint+pow". Under the second, a verifier with an old store accepts new headers only if the bundle carries every header from the store's tip to h+k−1 (80 B each). R-3.48's "embed the k-header segment" is enough only when the store is at most one block stale. The full mainnet header chain is about 77 MB (≈ 956,600 × 80 B), and growing about 4 MB a year |
