    28	## A7.1 Canonical payload bytes and the signed frame are two byte strings (C-03) — ADOPTED (author)
    29	
    30	*"I think it would be useful to clearly separate canonical payload
    31	bytes from the signed frame."*
    32	
    33	**Instrument.** Two terms, fixed: the **canonical payload bytes** are
    34	`canonical(payload)`, the JCS (RFC 8785) serialization under the
    35	precision rule of the original §4.3; the **signed frame** is the
    36	framed envelope of Amendment 1 §A1.2 P8, `type_tag ||
    37	canonicalization_version || payload_length || payload`, whose fourth
    38	field *is* the canonical payload bytes. Signatures and anchors commit
    39	to the signed frame. The length is a frame field. The original §4.3's
    40	sentence "Length is bound into the canonical bytes (not a side field)"
    41	(`docs/phase-0-prereg.md:492-494`) is superseded by A1 P8 as A1 §A1.8
    42	already implies, and is read from this amendment on as "the length is
    43	bound into the signed bytes"; it is not edited. Golden vectors exist
    44	for both strings separately (P8: the canonical payload bytes; the
    45	frame layout: the signed frame).
    46	
    47	## A7.2 The issuer-key manifest may carry references, resolvable only from the bundle or from declared trusted inputs (C-04) — ADOPTED (author), reading B
    48	
    49	*"B. You should allow references to be resolved entirely from the
    50	bundle or from explicitly declared trusted inputs. Note that a
    51	reference shouldn't require a network lookup (e.g., offline, albeit
    52	limitations are permissible.)"*
    53	
    54	**Instrument.** The original §4.4's "(or references)" stands. A
    55	reference in the manifest is permitted when its target is resolvable
    56	either (i) from material inside the bundle or (ii) from an input the
    57	verifier's declared policy explicitly names as trusted (the trust
    58	configuration of the implementation specification §2.3), and never by
    59	a live lookup. Under (ii) the reference stays inside P9's pure
    60	function, since the declared policy is one of its two arguments;
    61	"explicitly declared" was the collaborator's reading of how P9 is
    62	preserved, **confirmed by the author 2026-09-14:** *"Agreed. Make them
    63	explicitly declared."* A trusted input not named in the declared policy
    64	is not a trusted input. A reference the
    65	verifier cannot resolve from (i) or (ii) makes the check that needs
    66	it unperformable (`UNVERIFIABLE` for that required check, per the P4
    67	partition); "limitations are permissible" is read as this and nothing
    68	broader. A1 §A1.5's "archived in the bundle" for the two authority
    69	evidences is unchanged: (ii) does not license archiving an authority
    70	evidence outside the bundle.
    71	
    72	## A7.3 The scope of the `anchor-late` observation (C-05) — ADOPTED (author); reading confirmed by the author
    73	
    74	*"I'd suggest making the object scope explicit: the receipt's required
    75	temporal checks are still not non-waivable. This still permits a
    76	waived redundant authority to carry a late-anchor observation. I
    77	wouldn't permit any waiver to eliminate the final complete chain."*
    78	
    79	**Reading, confirmed by the author.** The second sentence as typed,
    80	"still not non-waivable", would make the receipt's own temporal checks
    81	waivable, which the third sentence, A2 §A2.2 ("Temporal-anchor
    82	consistency is already non-waivable") and A3 §A3.2's linked floor all
    83	forbid. The collaborator read it as *"still non-waivable"* and
    84	instrumented that. **Confirmed by the author, 2026-09-14, in session:**
    85	*"(1) was not intended to make them waivable."* The section stands.
    86	
    87	**Instrument.** Object scope, made explicit: A2 §A2.2's three
    88	temporal conjuncts apply to **the receipt's own shipped anchor** and
    89	are non-waivable; a late receipt anchor is performed-and-failed,
    90	`INVALID`, never an observation. A5 §A5.3's `anchor-late` is an
    91	observation recorded against **a waived redundant authority
    92	evidence's own anchor** and never determines the verdict. No waiver
    93	may remove the final complete evidence chain (A3 §A3.2, unchanged).
    94	A2 §A2.2 and A5 §A5.3 are both correct on their own objects; neither
    95	is edited.
