   327	## 5. Remaining work to 30 October
   328	
   329	Sizes are owner or agent effort. **[A]** marks a step that needs the author.
   330	
   331	1. **This draft and A9 reviewed.** Done means one full Codex pass and one skeptic read, every finding dispositioned in `docs/reviews/`. **0.5 d.** Due 12 Oct.
   332	2. **[A] A9 ruled and signed.** Done means Q1–Q5 answered and the author's commit containing A9. **About 1 h of author time.** Due 16 Oct.
   333	3. **[A] DNSSEC for D.** Done means the zone is signed (Route 53 with a KMS KSK, or the registrar's service), the DS record is published, `dig +dnssec @1.1.1.1` shows the AD flag, and the key TXT record is live. **About 1 h of author time, plus 1–2 days of propagation.** Start this week: if the registrar cannot do DNSSEC, a transfer takes days to weeks.
   334	4. **Git channel.** Done means V1 keys published in a signed, OTS-anchored commit, and that commit verified offline by `gitchannel.py`. **2 h, plus [A] the commit.**
   335	5. **V1 profile document** (E16 extract). Done means the bundle format, profile id, strict verifier stages (a)–(h), trust configuration and the A9.1 expectation table, with each requirement tagged to its source and no more than about 400 lines. **2 d.**
   336	6. **P8.** Done means a prose proof of injectivity and boundary-unambiguity over the V1 domain, the O-79 map, golden and rejection vectors on which two implementations agree, and a Codex falsification run. **4 d**, plus **[A] a 30-min plain-language read**.
   337	7. **Spec fixes.** Done means dated, tagged edits to the profile and the spec, covering:
   338	   - W1;
   339	   - G-21 owner name, with the M2 statement;
   340	   - T = the earliest verified attestation, V33a, and the header rule (PoW + linkage + checkpoint);
   341	   - `revocation: NOT_EVALUATED`;
   342	   - the verifier-fixed required set (G-17);
   343	   - M5;
   344	   - DNSSEC at T (M8);
   345	   - an argument that no strict capstone query relies on signatures being unique.
   346	
   347	   Plus one Codex review. **2.5 d.**
   348	8. **E6a.** Done means the P4 out-of-profile / unknown-algorithm transition, with a fail-open companion red and the witnesses firing. **0.5 d.**
   349	9. **E4 (P9).** Done means an inspection artifact for the V1 verifier and the two-machine vector specified. **0.5 d.**
   350	10. **E8, E7, E11.** Done means a V1-scoped correspondence mapping, a mapping sentence for each V1 query, and one index page. **2 d.**
   351	11. **E12.** Done means the one-page relying-party story for V1, covering revocation, standing, the submitter, out-of-profile bundles and the ten scenarios. **1 d**, plus **[A] a 30-min read**.
   352	12. **E10 specification.** Done means the list of trace-to-fixture vectors and the concretizer design, reviewed by Codex. Extraction runs by 13 Nov. **1 d.**
   353	13. **E18.** Done means Codex's full pass on the k = 6 evidence. **0.5 d.**
   354	14. **Slice batch.** Done means the batch finishes and gets a read note of one page or less, filed as design-level evidence. **0.5 d.** No further capstone work for V1.
   355	15. **[A] E9.** Done means one assembled file with each V1 row's surviving findings and its statement, the author's adjudications entered, and V1 rows marked `discharged`. **1 d of assembly, plus about 2 h of author time.** Due about 27 Oct.
   356	16. **[A] E17.** Done means the signed V1-scoped exit commit that opens Phase 1a for V1. **10 min.** Due 30 Oct.
   357	17. **[A] AWS monthly cap set.** **10 min.**
   358	
   359	Total: about 19 owner-days in 15 working days. That works only with three parallel agents (Sonnet as core, Opus for the P8 proof and reviews). Author time: about 5–6 h over three weeks, which is the whole of the 2 h a week budget.
   360	
   361	---
   362	
   363	## 6. Blunt findings
   364	
   365	1. **Revocation forces an explicit weakening.** Under the registered P4 and P6, a verifier that cannot evaluate revocation has to return `UNVERIFIABLE` for every receipt. "Not evaluated" is therefore a property weakening under A1 §A1.1, not a scope note. If the author refuses Q3 (a), V1 either says nothing useful (b) or misses 30 Oct (c).
   366	2. **The success definition fails on V1's own scenario set as written.** Case (e) expects `INVALID` from a revocation, (a) expects standing `ESTABLISHED`, and (h) expects a wrapper to verify. V1 cannot produce any of these. Their expectations have to be re-registered by 16 Oct, before any V1 run, or this will look like moving the goalposts. Ten cases are also thin evidence for "every case". The model-derived vectors (E10) should be counted as the larger set.
   367	3. **The DNS channel does not exist yet.** wamason.com is unsigned today. This is the one 30 Oct dependency that only the author can start, and it can take more than a week.
   368	4. **The author's time is the long pole, not the models.** A9, the DNSSEC set-up, three reads and E9 use his entire budget until 30 Oct. Any extra routed question slips the milestone.
   369	5. **Who the relying-party tester is has not been defined.** The 4 Dec test needs a named non-expert integrator. If the tester is a model, say so in A9.1: the owner recommends a fresh non-author model with the documentation only, plus a human if one is available.
   370	6. **Is V1 worthwhile?** As a demonstration, yes. It gives a formally grounded, offline, fail-closed verification of bytes, signature, authority and time. Commercially it is thin. Without revocation, standing or renewal, a V1 receipt says little more than OTS plus two detached signatures plus archived DNSSEC and git evidence. Its differentiator is the documented safe decisions and verification with Tessera gone. It is worthwhile only if A9.3's T4 path (LTV and renewal) is real and A9.4's test holds. Otherwise V1 is a demo with a shelf life.
   371	7. **Latency is measured in hours.** k = 6 confirmations plus OTS calendar aggregation, with up to 12.6 h between attestations observed, means the "stated latency" will be hours, bounded by δ = 72 h. It has to be stated that way.
